        "description": "<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/ef3bd8a7-35d8-46c6-b73d-6df0e91906dd,1794597246129/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C81%2C1024%2C576%29%3B' alt='A person wearing sunglasses stands in front of people waving flags and Antigua and Barbuda' width='620' height='349' title='Britain&apos;s King Charles III greets locals holding national flags during a visit to Nelson&apos;s Dockyard, a historic naval base established in 1725, in St. John&apos;s, Antigua and Barbuda, on November 2, 2026. (Photo by Isabel Infantes / POOL / AFP via Getty Images)'/><p>As with trips to Canada in 2025 and the United States earlier this year, King Charles's recent visit to the Caribbean saw him walking something of a diplomatic tightrope at a politically charged time. There seems to be an emerging pattern.</p>",
